   "text": " the exhibit contained \"numerous examples of 'unidentified' objects that were likely sold out and then sold into the market for scrap metal, which was then passed on to collectors through a number of different ways\" by collectors, including through the auction house. The exhibit \"should be considered an artifact of interest to collectors that have seen it displayed for the last 25 years,\" the commission found, citing a description of a \"lone wolf creature.\"\n\nHowever, the commission concludes that the exhibit \"has no potential to become a permanent part of historical collections.\"\n\nThe commission also noted that it was \"particularly important to note\" that the exhibit does not depict an alien civilization by means of traditional scientific findings like fossils or genetic material to be examined by archaeologists.\n\nStill, some of the exhibits may prove invaluable to the archaeologist's career. On Dec. 12, 2013, Ulloa said a small piece of a human anatomy specimen was removed from a wall at an archaeological site in the northwest part of Montana that he used as a table. The piece remained at the site for nearly three years until archaeologists finally found it in mid-December. Ulloa, who is a professor at the University of Alaska Fairbanks in the United States, said the collection included a human skull in the wall and an animal tooth as part of the scene.\n\nThis episode marks one day in a decade of digging, excavations and discovery that has produced the first, and possibly most, human skulls ever found in one of the nation's most isolated places of archaeological interest.\n\nA pair",
